## Context

Stage 3 of the pipeline. Snapshot formats over the 115 unextracted documents:
HTML 66, PDF 38, octet-stream 6, xlsx 2, JSON 2, JavaScript 2. The existing
PDF parsers open files their own way: four use pdfplumber (pinned 0.11.9), one
shells out to `pdftotext -layout`, and no text is cached. Ticket [[1512]]
(deferred, "take it up when a new parser is written") asks for a shared text
layer; take it up here rather than duplicate it, and close or fold it in.

## Work

- One adapter per format returning a common structure (pages or sections with
  stable locators) from bytes, so an extractor never opens a file itself.
- A text cache keyed by `sha256` and adapter version.
- Content-type sniffing for the octet-stream objects.
- Existing parsers keep their layout logic; they are ported to the adapter
  output, not rewritten. Their outputs stay byte-identical (checked by 1507).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Every format in the 369 snapshots is handled by an adapter or named as not handled with its reason
- [ ] The five existing PDF parsers read through the shared layer, or the reason one cannot (for example `pdftotext -layout` for Senelec) is recorded
- [ ] Parser outputs byte-identical before and after the port
- [ ] A corrupt or empty file yields a disposition, not a crash (red test)
- [ ] `make jetp-ledger-check`, `make check-fast`, `make lint` and `make check-jetp` pass
